well i think today's figures show  actually that our economy is clearly  getting back on track in particular if  you look at the numbers  for march but crucially they show that  our plan for jobs  is working and i think that's the  important thing that i take away from  today's figures  look of course many households and  families are still facing tough times  and that's why  our support is continuing restrictions  will start to ease further on  monday be able to come inside places  like this for a drink and further on  june the 21st but you'll know  the hospitality industry particularly  wants more certainty if restrictions  don't entirely go  will some economic support remain well  we provide a considerable support  to the hospitality industry through this  crisis and that support  continues and it continues well beyond  the end of the road map and the business  we're in today is a great example  of that where they've been able to  furlough staff but are now  brilliantly brought them back to work  they're also taking on new  employees a kickstarter which is  fantastic and they've benefited  from discretionary cash grants and other  hospitality benefit  businesses have benefited from the vat  cut or indeed business rates holidays  so you know i know it's a tough time for  the hospitality industry but the crucial  thing is we're making progress on the  road map next week will be a big week  and i want to bring as many of those  furloughed workers back to work  and that's what we're seeing in today's  figures our plan for jobs is working  if social distancing in any form or any  restrictions remain and 100 capacity  can't be reached that's the difference  between  being viable and not so will you  leave some support if you don't remove  all the restrictions you've placed on  business  well i think i'd say two things the  prime minister's said that we're in  good shape to continue making progress  on the road map so i think that should  fill  uh people with some confidence and  optimism about the future  but also our support continues beyond  the end of the roadmap whether it's  business rates  reductions or vat cuts or furlough  all those things continue well beyond  the end of the roadmap so that support  is in place  last summer you stimulated spending eat  out to help out you planning anything  similar are you just going to trust  consumers  to get out there and spend the money  though so so i think as a result of the  actions that we've taken over the past  year we've managed to protect a lot of  household incomes and you see that in  the recent economic figures  and i think that bodes well for the rest  of the year  our plan is working consumers have built  up savings obviously that's not  evenly distributed and what we now know  which we didn't know a year ago that  actually as things open up  people do want to get out and go back to  doing the things that they used to do  and i think we will see that in the  coming weeks and months as we are  already seeing it  in today's figures which show that our  plan is working can they trust though  that you're not  going to knock down the economy again  can can they trust to spend those  savings  the risk that you might close things  down again well i think people can see  with the success of the vaccine roll out  and with as the prime minister explained  earlier this week as well you know we  are meeting all the tests for progress  on  the roadmap and people are doing a  fantastic job  of following all the rules and that's  enabled us to  bit by bit reopen our economy reduce the  restrictions people go back to doing the  things that they love doing i think  that's  enormously positive and that's why we're  seeing whether it's consumer confidence  which is returned  to pre-crisis levels business confidence  you know there's an enormous amount of  optimism about the future  and you know i take a lot of heart from  that and it makes me think that the plan  we put in place a year ago  is working should people go back to the  office and if so when  i said obviously you know we'll get to  later stages of the road map  later that is a decision for employees  and employers  and you know i would imagine as many  people are having those conversations  about some degree of flexibility and  work arrangements that's absolutely  right that those conversations should  happen around four million people at  least still on furlough  how many of them do you think will be  unemployed come the end of the year  well what we do know now is that  furlough has worked in protecting  millions of people's jobs over the past  year  and if you look at the unemployment rate  for example here in the uk  it is lower than most of our major  competitors whether that's america  canada france spain  italy and that's important to me because  protecting people's jobs is my number  one priority  you know and i believe that as we reopen  the economy we can get as  many of those people back to work as  possible um how many is that how many is  what  well as the economy reopens we'll see  but i've always been clear not every  single job  is going to be one that we can save but  that's why it's important that we  provide new opportunities for people  whether it's through our  apprenticeship bonuses the kickstart  scheme the prime minister's lifetime  skills guarantee  all of those provide fresh opportunity  and hope for people  and that's what we will deliver on this  year how are you going to pay for social  care  i think the government remains committed  to supporting the social care system and  as we set out in our manifesto we  already are an  extra billion pounds a year has already  been invested  into the social care system
 

